Fill the complete group in one response. The XML template defines required
structure; the complete translation payload defines delivered content.

Reading-order example:
Source: <xml><fragment id="1">The main sentence starts</fragment><fragment id="2">Sidebar.</fragment><fragment id="3">and ends here.</fragment></xml>
Translation: "La frase principal empieza y termina aquí. Nota."
WRONG: <xml><fragment id="1">La frase principal empieza</fragment><fragment id="2">Nota.</fragment><fragment id="3">y termina aquí.</fragment></xml>
CORRECT: <xml><fragment id="1">La frase principal empieza y termina aquí.</fragment><fragment id="2">Nota.</fragment><fragment id="3"/></xml>
Move the entire translated passage ahead of the sidebar, even though its source
was split across blocks 1 and 3. The later block retains its identity EMPTY.
Source semantic matching loses to translation order; source length is not a
capacity limit. Do not relocate the sidebar inside the main translated passage.
Returning block 3 before block 2 does not move those fixed output positions.

Formatting does not freeze source spelling:
Source: <p id="1">the <code id="2">return</code> operands</p>
Translation: "os operandos de retorno"
CORRECT: <p id="1">os operandos de <code id="2">retorno</code></p>
Do not replace "retorno" with "return" to make it look like valid source code.
The tag and ID survive; their text follows the artist, even inside code.

Formula containers preserve formatting, not the source numeric value:
Source: <p id="1"><expression id="2">3</expression> billion</p>
Translation: "30亿"
CORRECT: <p id="1"><expression id="2">30</expression>亿</p>
Do not restore 3. Do not empty the formula and put all its text outside it when
the translated numeric content belongs in that existing formula container.

One translated formula can span several source formula containers:
Translation: "$u=v+w$"; required expression IDs: 2 and 3.
CORRECT: <p id="1"><expression id="2">$u=v+w$</expression><expression id="3"/></p>
ALSO CORRECT: <p id="1"><expression id="2">$u=</expression><expression id="3">v+w$</expression></p>
WRONG: <p id="1"><expression id="2">$u=$</expression><expression id="3">$v+w$</expression></p>
The last version inserts two dollar signs. A source container is not a separate
mathematical boundary: never add delimiters to complete each container's formula.

Repetitions belong to the translation too, including repetitions that look
accidental. Do not deduplicate them or infer their count from source blocks.
For source <xml><p id="1">Echo.</p><p id="2">Echo.</p></xml> and translation
"Echo. Echo. Echo.", a complete fill is
<xml><p id="1">Echo. Echo.</p><p id="2">Echo.</p></xml>.
One phrase per source container would lose an occurrence. The same applies to
repeated punctuation. Keep the source containers, not their original lengths.

The number of translated formulas is not the number of source expression nodes.
For required IDs 2 and 3 and translation "$x$ $x$ $x$", this preserves all content:
<p id="1"><expression id="2"/><expression id="3">$x$ $x$ $x$</expression></p>
For those SAME required IDs and translation "$x$", this is complete:
<p id="1"><expression id="3"/><expression id="2">$x$</expression></p>
Here the unused empty inline ID 3 is emitted first, before the filled ID 2,
inside their original parent. This changes no visible text and leaves no empty
inline identities to emit after the long passage. Do not do this with blocks or
resource anchors.
Do not force the artist to repeat twice merely because there are two source
containers. Do not shorten three translated occurrences to two either. Every
occurrence counts, even when the artist repeats a passage hundreds of times or
appears to have made a generation mistake. You are not the artist's editor.
